/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Jeremiah chapters 16-20
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("jeremiah", [
{n:16, name:"The Sign of Sorrow", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 16:21",verse:"Therefore, behold, I will this once cause them to know, I will cause them to know mine hand and my might; and they shall know that my name is The LORD."}, facts:["Did you know Jeremiah was commanded never to marry or have children, because the children born in Jerusalem would die of grievous deaths (Jeremiah 16:2, 4)?","Did you know this chapter looks past the exile to a second exodus, when people would swear by the God who brought them from the land of the north instead of the God who brought them out of Egypt (Jeremiah 16:14-15)?","Did you know Jeremiah foresaw Gentiles coming from the ends of the earth, confessing that their fathers had inherited lies and vanity (Jeremiah 16:19)?"], questions:[
{q:"What was Jeremiah forbidden to do in this place?", options:["Not take a wife, nor have sons or daughters","Not speak to the priests","Not enter the temple","Not leave Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:2",verse:"Thou shalt not take thee a wife, neither shalt thou have sons or daughters in this place.", insight:"God sometimes asks His servants to carry the coming sorrow in their own lives; Jeremiah's empty house preached louder than words."},
{q:"For whom was this word of judgment spoken?", options:["The sons and daughters born in this place, and their mothers and fathers","Only the kings of Judah","Only the priests of the temple","The merchants of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:3",verse:"For thus saith the LORD concerning the sons and concerning the daughters that are born in this place, and concerning their mothers that bare them, and concerning their fathers that begat them in this land;", insight:"Judgment named every generation, children, mothers, fathers; no household could claim it was someone else's sin."},
{q:"What fate did God say awaited the children born in this place?", options:["They shall die of grievous deaths","They would be carried into Egypt","They would live to old age in peace","They would become priests"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:4",verse:"They shall die of grievous deaths; they shall not be lamented; neither shall they be buried; but they shall be as dung upon the face of the earth: and they shall be consumed by the sword, and by famine; and their carcases shall be meat for the fowls of heaven, and for the beasts of the earth.", insight:"The judgment is terrible because the sin is terrible; God never threatens lightly, and His warnings are meant to turn hearts."},
{q:"What house was Jeremiah told not to enter?", options:["The house of mourning","The house of the king","The house of his father","The gate of the city"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:5",verse:"For thus saith the LORD, Enter not into the house of mourning, neither go to lament nor bemoan them: for I have taken away my peace from this people, saith the LORD, even lovingkindness and mercies.", insight:"When God has taken away His peace, mourning rituals are empty theatre; Jeremiah must not pretend otherwise."},
{q:"Who would die in this land?", options:["Both the great and the small","Only the priests and kings","Only the soldiers","Only the elders"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:6",verse:"Both the great and the small shall die in this land: they shall not be buried, neither shall men lament for them, nor cut themselves, nor make themselves bald for them:", insight:"Judgment that spares no rank is a reminder that sin has no rank either."},
{q:"What would no man give in mourning?", options:["The cup of consolation to drink","Bread to the hungry","Water to the thirsty","Money to the poor"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:7",verse:"Neither shall men tear themselves for them in mourning, to comfort them for the dead; neither shall men give them the cup of consolation to drink for their father or for their mother.", insight:"Even the small kindness of a shared cup would be gone; judgment dries up mercy between neighbors."},
{q:"What second house must Jeremiah avoid?", options:["The house of feasting","The house of prayer","The house of his neighbor","The house of the scribe"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:8",verse:"Thou shalt not also go into the house of feasting, to sit with them to eat and to drink.", insight:"No weddings while the city stands sentenced; Jeremiah's absence from the feast was itself the sermon."},
{q:"What four voices would God cause to cease?", options:["The voice of mirth, the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom, and the voice of the bride","The voice of the king, the priests, the prophets, and the people","The voice of singing, dancing, shouting, and laughter","The voice of the trumpet, the harp, the pipe, and the drum"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:9",verse:"For thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Behold, I will cause to cease out of this place in your eyes, and in your days, the voice of mirth, and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom, and the voice of the bride.", insight:"When the bridegroom's voice goes silent, a whole future has been cancelled."},
{q:"What question would the people ask Jeremiah?", options:["Wherefore hath the LORD pronounced all this great evil against us?","When will the king return to the city?","Who will lead us into battle?","How shall we rebuild the walls?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:10",verse:"And it shall come to pass, when thou shalt shew this people all these words, and they shall say unto thee, Wherefore hath the LORD pronounced all this great evil against us? or what is our iniquity? or what is our sin that we have committed against the LORD our God?", insight:"The guilty always ask why first; Jeremiah's answer would not flatter them."},
{q:"What had their fathers done?", options:["Forsaken me, and walked after other gods","Built the temple too slowly","Neglected the sabbath","Oppressed the poor alone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:11",verse:"Then shalt thou say unto them, Because your fathers have forsaken me, saith the LORD, and have walked after other gods, and have served them, and have worshipped them, and have forsaken me, and have not kept my law;", insight:"Every fall begins the same way: forsaking God, then following something else."},
{q:"How had this generation outdone their fathers?", options:["They had done worse, walking every one after the imagination of his evil heart","They had built more altars than before","They had forgotten the law entirely","They had crowned a foreign king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:12",verse:"And ye have done worse than your fathers; for, behold, ye walk every one after the imagination of his evil heart, that they may not hearken unto me:", insight:"Sin compounds; each generation that will not listen walks further than the last."},
{q:"Where would God cast them?", options:["Into a land that ye know not, neither ye nor your fathers","Into the wilderness of Sinai","Into the land of Egypt again","Into the mountains of Moab"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:13",verse:"Therefore will I cast you out of this land into a land that ye know not, neither ye nor your fathers; and there shall ye serve other gods day and night; where I will not shew you favour.", insight:"Exile mirrors idolatry: they loved foreign gods, so they would live in a foreign land."},
{q:"What would no more be said?", options:["The LORD liveth, that brought up the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt","The LORD liveth, that gave us the law at Sinai","The LORD liveth, that chose David our king","The LORD liveth, that built us this temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:14",verse:"Therefore, behold, the days come, saith the LORD, that it shall no more be said, The LORD liveth, that brought up the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt;", insight:"God was about to do something so great that the exodus would no longer be His people's favorite story."},
{q:"What new saying would replace it?", options:["The LORD liveth, that brought up the children of Israel from the land of the north","The LORD liveth, that scattered our enemies","The LORD liveth, that rebuilt our walls","The LORD liveth, that sent us prophets"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:15",verse:"But, The LORD liveth, that brought up the children of Israel from the land of the north, and from all the lands whither he had driven them: and I will bring them again into their land that I gave unto their fathers.", insight:"The return from exile would become the new exodus; God's rescue is never a one time story."},
{q:"Whom would God send first to gather them?", options:["Many fishers","Many shepherds","Many builders","Many singers"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:16",verse:"Behold, I will send for many fishers, saith the LORD, and they shall fish them; and after will I send for many hunters, and they shall hunt them from every mountain, and from every hill, and out of the holes of the rocks.", insight:"Before the hunters come, the fishers; God gathers before He judges."},
{q:"What did God say about His eyes?", options:["Mine eyes are upon all their ways","Mine eyes are closed to their prayers","Mine eyes look only on the temple","Mine eyes have seen their riches"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:17",verse:"For mine eyes are upon all their ways: they are not hid from my face, neither is their iniquity hid from mine eyes.", insight:"Nothing is hid from His face; that is terror to the guilty and comfort to the faithful."},
{q:"Why would God recompense their iniquity double?", options:["Because they defiled His land and filled His inheritance with detestable things","Because they broke the sabbath","Because they refused to pay tribute","Because they trusted in Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:18",verse:"And first I will recompense their iniquity and their sin double; because they have defiled my land, they have filled mine inheritance with the carcases of their detestable and abominable things.", insight:"Idolatry is not private; it defiles the land God gave as an inheritance."},
{q:"What would the Gentiles confess?", options:["Surely our fathers have inherited lies, vanity, and things wherein there is no profit","Surely the gods of the nations are strong","Surely Israel's God is like our gods","Surely the prophets were mistaken"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:19",verse:"O LORD, my strength, and my fortress, and my refuge in the day of affliction, the Gentiles shall come unto thee from the ends of the earth, and shall say, Surely our fathers have inherited lies, vanity, and things wherein there is no profit.", insight:"The day is coming when the nations themselves will call their idols lies; truth outlives every false god."},
{q:"What question does Jeremiah ask about idols?", options:["Shall a man make gods unto himself, and they are no gods?","Shall a man worship what his fathers worshipped?","Shall a man trust in his own strength?","Shall a man forget the law of God?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:20",verse:"Shall a man make gods unto himself, and they are no gods?", insight:"A god you made is no god at all; the question answers itself."},
{q:"What would God cause them to know?", options:["His hand and His might, and that His name is The LORD","The names of their enemies","The number of their sins","The length of their exile"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 16:21",verse:"Therefore, behold, I will this once cause them to know, I will cause them to know mine hand and my might; and they shall know that my name is The LORD.", insight:"In the end, judgment is revelation: they would know, beyond all doubt, who the LORD is."}
]},
{n:17, name:"The Deceitful Heart", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 17:9",verse:"The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked: who can know it?"}, facts:["Did you know this chapter compares the trusting heart to a tree planted by the waters, which stays green in heat and keeps yielding fruit in a drought (Jeremiah 17:7-8)?","Did you know the chapter holds one of Scripture's most quoted sentences: 'The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked' (Jeremiah 17:9)?","Did you know Jerusalem's survival was tied to keeping the sabbath? Obedience meant kings on David's throne forever; breaking it meant fire in the gates that would not be quenched (Jeremiah 17:24-27)."], questions:[
{q:"With what was the sin of Judah written?", options:["A pen of iron, with the point of a diamond","A reed pen with black ink","A stylus of bronze","Charcoal upon parchment"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:1",verse:"The sin of Judah is written with a pen of iron, and with the point of a diamond: it is graven upon the table of their heart, and upon the horns of your altars;", insight:"Sin etched with iron cannot be wiped away with excuses; only repentance reaches it."},
{q:"What did their children remember?", options:["Their altars and their groves by the green trees upon the high hills","The stories of the exodus","The songs of the temple","The laws of Moses"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:2",verse:"Whilst their children remember their altars and their groves by the green trees upon the high hills.", insight:"Children inherit what parents practice; idolatry was being passed down like a family trade."},
{q:"What would God give to the spoil?", options:["Their substance and all their treasures","Their vineyards and olive groves","Their flocks and herds","Their sons and daughters"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:3",verse:"O my mountain in the field, I will give thy substance and all thy treasures to the spoil, and thy high places for sin, throughout all thy borders.", insight:"Wealth stored up in disobedience becomes plunder; what we clutch, God can scatter."},
{q:"What would happen to their heritage?", options:["They would discontinue from it and serve their enemies in a land they knew not","It would be divided among the tribes","It would be given to the priests","It would lie fallow for seven years"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:4",verse:"And thou, even thyself, shalt discontinue from thine heritage that I gave thee; and I will cause thee to serve thine enemies in the land which thou knowest not: for ye have kindled a fire in mine anger, which shall burn for ever.", insight:"The land was a gift, not a possession; gifts can be taken back."},
{q:"Who is cursed?", options:["The man that trusteth in man, and maketh flesh his arm","The man who builds a house of cedar","The man who plants a vineyard","The man who travels to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:5",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; Cursed be the man that trusteth in man, and maketh flesh his arm, and whose heart departeth from the LORD.", insight:"Trust placed in human strength is a curse, because human strength always fails."},
{q:"What is the cursed man like?", options:["The heath in the desert","A tree by the river","A vine on the wall","Grass on the housetop"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:6",verse:"For he shall be like the heath in the desert, and shall not see when good cometh; but shall inhabit the parched places in the wilderness, in a salt land and not inhabited.", insight:"A shrub in a salt land: alive, but barely; that is life without God."},
{q:"Who is blessed?", options:["The man that trusteth in the LORD, and whose hope the LORD is","The man who keeps the king's favor","The man who stores up grain","The man who dwells in Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:7",verse:"Blessed is the man that trusteth in the LORD, and whose hope the LORD is.", insight:"Blessing follows trust the way a shadow follows a tree; put your hope where it cannot rot."},
{q:"What is the blessed man like?", options:["A tree planted by the waters, spreading out her roots by the river","A cedar on Lebanon","An olive tree in the court of God","A palm tree in the desert"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:8",verse:"For he shall be as a tree planted by the waters, and that spreadeth out her roots by the river, and shall not see when heat cometh, but her leaf shall be green; and shall not be careful in the year of drought, neither shall cease from yielding fruit.", insight:"Roots by the river do not panic in a drought; hidden trust feeds visible fruit."},
{q:"What is the heart?", options:["Deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked","Pure and easily guided","Strong and always trustworthy","Simple and easily taught"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:9",verse:"The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked: who can know it?", insight:"The most dangerous liar you will ever meet is your own heart; let God search it."},
{q:"What does the LORD do?", options:["Searches the heart, tries the reins, and gives every man according to his ways","Counts the stars and names them","Weighs the mountains in a balance","Numbers the hairs of the head"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:10",verse:"I the LORD search the heart, I try the reins, even to give every man according to his ways, and according to the fruit of his doings.", insight:"God grades by the heart and pays by the fruit; both are exactly fair."},
{q:"What happens to riches gotten not by right?", options:["He shall leave them in the midst of his days, and at his end shall be a fool","They will double in his old age","They will be given to his children forever","They will buy him honor among men"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:11",verse:"As the partridge sitteth on eggs, and hatcheth them not; so he that getteth riches, and not by right, shall leave them in the midst of his days, and at his end shall be a fool.", insight:"Dishonest gain has an expiry date; it leaves before you do."},
{q:"What is the place of our sanctuary?", options:["A glorious high throne from the beginning","A tent in the wilderness","A cave in the mountains","A garden by the river"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:12",verse:"A glorious high throne from the beginning is the place of our sanctuary.", insight:"God's throne predates every trouble; the sanctuary stands on something unshakable."},
{q:"What have the forsakers left?", options:["The LORD, the fountain of living waters","The city of their fathers","The temple of Solomon","The covenant of peace"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:13",verse:"O LORD, the hope of Israel, all that forsake thee shall be ashamed, and they that depart from me shall be written in the earth, because they have forsaken the LORD, the fountain of living waters.", insight:"They traded a fountain for cracked cisterns; no wonder they are thirsty."},
{q:"What does Jeremiah pray?", options:["Heal me, O LORD, and I shall be healed; save me, and I shall be saved","Give me riches and honor","Send me to a far country","Hide me from my enemies"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:14",verse:"Heal me, O LORD, and I shall be healed; save me, and I shall be saved: for thou art my praise.", insight:"Two short prayers cover the whole need: a healed body and a saved soul."},
{q:"What did the mockers say?", options:["Where is the word of the LORD? let it come now","Who is this prophet?","When will the king listen?","Why does God delay?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:15",verse:"Behold, they say unto me, Where is the word of the LORD? let it come now.", insight:"Mockers demand proof on their schedule; God's word keeps its own."},
{q:"What does Jeremiah say about his calling?", options:["I have not hastened from being a pastor to follow thee","I chose this work myself","I desired the woeful day","I spoke only my own words"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:16",verse:"As for me, I have not hastened from being a pastor to follow thee: neither have I desired the woeful day; thou knowest: that which came out of my lips was right before thee.", insight:"He never ran ahead of God and never wished for the disaster he preached; that is a true pastor."},
{q:"What does Jeremiah ask for his persecutors?", options:["Bring upon them the day of evil, and destroy them with double destruction","Forgive them, for they know not","Bless them with long life","Send them into exile"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:18",verse:"Let them be confounded that persecute me, but let not me be confounded: let them be dismayed, but let not me be dismayed: bring upon them the day of evil, and destroy them with double destruction.", insight:"A hard prayer from a wounded prophet; even God's servants sometimes cry for justice before mercy."},
{q:"Where was Jeremiah told to stand?", options:["In the gate of the children of the people, where the kings of Judah come in and go out","On the steps of the temple","In the middle of the marketplace","At the door of the palace"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:19",verse:"Thus said the LORD unto me; Go and stand in the gate of the children of the people, whereby the kings of Judah come in, and by the which they go out, and in all the gates of Jerusalem;", insight:"God's word belongs where the traffic is; truth is not whispered in corners."},
{q:"What sabbath command was given?", options:["Bear no burden on the sabbath day, nor bring it in by the gates of Jerusalem","Offer double sacrifices","Close the temple doors","Gather manna twice"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:21",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; Take heed to yourselves, and bear no burden on the sabbath day, nor bring it in by the gates of Jerusalem;", insight:"One day of rest was a weekly sermon: God, not your labor, keeps you."},
{q:"What would happen if they would not hallow the sabbath?", options:["He would kindle a fire in the gates that would devour the palaces and not be quenched","He would send a famine for seven years","He would give the city to the Philistines","He would silence the prophets"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 17:27",verse:"But if ye will not hearken unto me to hallow the sabbath day, and not to bear a burden, even entering in at the gates of Jerusalem on the sabbath day; then will I kindle a fire in the gates thereof, and it shall devour the palaces of Jerusalem, and it shall not be quenched.", insight:"A small disobedience, repeated weekly, can burn a city; God measures the sabbath by the heart behind it."}
]},
{n:18, name:"The Potter's House", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 18:6",verse:"O house of Israel, cannot I do with you as this potter? saith the LORD. Behold, as the clay is in the potter’s hand, so are ye in mine hand, O house of Israel."}, facts:["Did you know the potter's house parable turns on repentance? God may announce judgment and relent if the nation turns, or announce blessing and withdraw it if the nation turns evil (Jeremiah 18:7-10).","Did you know Jeremiah's reply to the marred vessel is one of Scripture's clearest pictures of God's sovereignty: 'as the clay is in the potter's hand, so are ye in mine hand' (Jeremiah 18:6)?","Did you know this chapter ends with one of the hardest prayers in the Bible, Jeremiah asking God not to forgive his persecutors (Jeremiah 18:23)?"], questions:[
{q:"Where was Jeremiah sent?", options:["Down to the potter's house","Up to the temple court","Out to the valley of Hinnom","Into the king's palace"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:2",verse:"Arise, and go down to the potter’s house, and there I will cause thee to hear my words.", insight:"God teaches in workshops, not just sanctuaries; go where the clay is being shaped."},
{q:"What was the potter doing?", options:["He wrought a work on the wheels","He dug clay from the pit","He painted finished vessels","He sold pots in the market"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:3",verse:"Then I went down to the potter’s house, and, behold, he wrought a work on the wheels.", insight:"God was already at work before Jeremiah arrived; revelation often means watching first."},
{q:"What happened to the vessel?", options:["It was marred in the potter's hand, so he made it again another vessel","It shattered and was thrown away","It was sold to a merchant","It was set aside unfinished"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:4",verse:"And the vessel that he made of clay was marred in the hand of the potter: so he made it again another vessel, as seemed good to the potter to make it.", insight:"A marred vessel is not a finished story; the Potter remakes what we would discard."},
{q:"What did God ask?", options:["Cannot I do with you as this potter?","Will you not learn from the clay?","Shall the vessel judge the maker?","Who taught the potter his craft?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:6",verse:"O house of Israel, cannot I do with you as this potter? saith the LORD. Behold, as the clay is in the potter’s hand, so are ye in mine hand, O house of Israel.", insight:"The question is the sermon: we are clay, and clay does not negotiate with the potter."},
{q:"What three things may God speak against a nation?", options:["To pluck up, and to pull down, and to destroy it","To tax, to enslave, and to scatter","To warn, to wait, and to forgive","To humble, to heal, and to restore"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:7",verse:"At what instant I shall speak concerning a nation, and concerning a kingdom, to pluck up, and to pull down, and to destroy it;", insight:"God's judgments are thorough because sin is thorough; nothing rotten is left standing."},
{q:"What turns aside God's pronounced evil?", options:["If that nation turn from their evil","If they offer more sacrifices","If they build a new temple","If they crown a righteous king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:8",verse:"If that nation, against whom I have pronounced, turn from their evil, I will repent of the evil that I thought to do unto them.", insight:"Repentance is the one thing that moves God's announced judgment; He repents of the evil when we repent of ours."},
{q:"What two blessings may God speak over a nation?", options:["To build and to plant it","To enrich and to enlarge","To crown and to conquer","To prosper and to protect"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:9",verse:"And at what instant I shall speak concerning a nation, and concerning a kingdom, to build and to plant it;", insight:"God's first instinct toward a nation is construction, not demolition."},
{q:"What cancels the good God promised?", options:["If it do evil in my sight, and obey not my voice","If the king dies young","If the harvest fails","If enemies invade"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:10",verse:"If it do evil in my sight, that it obey not my voice, then I will repent of the good, wherewith I said I would benefit them.", insight:"Promises of blessing are not blank checks; obedience keeps the account open."},
{q:"What was Judah told to do?", options:["Return every one from his evil way, and make your ways and your doings good","Flee to the mountains","Gather weapons for war","Pay tribute to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:11",verse:"Now therefore go to, speak to the men of Judah, and to the inhabitants of Jerusalem, saying, Thus saith the LORD; Behold, I frame evil against you, and devise a device against you: return ye now every one from his evil way, and make your ways and your doings good.", insight:"The way back is always the same road: turn, and then walk straight."},
{q:"How did the people answer?", options:["There is no hope: we will walk after our own devices","We will obey the prophet","We will fast and pray","We will ask the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:12",verse:"And they said, There is no hope: but we will walk after our own devices, and we will every one do the imagination of his evil heart.", insight:"'No hope' is the saddest sentence in the chapter; despair became their excuse for disobedience."},
{q:"Who had done a very horrible thing?", options:["The virgin of Israel","The daughters of Moab","The women of Samaria","The wives of the kings"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:13",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD; Ask ye now among the heathen, who hath heard such things: the virgin of Israel hath done a very horrible thing.", insight:"Calling Israel 'virgin' makes the betrayal sting; this was a bride's unfaithfulness."},
{q:"What does a man not leave, in Jeremiah's parable?", options:["The snow of Lebanon, nor the cold flowing waters","The shade of the cedars","The paths of the valley","The wells of his fathers"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:14",verse:"Will a man leave the snow of Lebanon which cometh from the rock of the field? or shall the cold flowing waters that come from another place be forsaken?", insight:"Nature stays constant, but God's people abandoned what never changes for what never satisfies."},
{q:"What had God's people forgotten?", options:["Me, burning incense to vanity and stumbling from the ancient paths","The sabbath and the feasts","The exodus and the wilderness","The covenant with David"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:15",verse:"Because my people hath forgotten me, they have burned incense to vanity, and they have caused them to stumble in their ways from the ancient paths, to walk in paths, in a way not cast up;", insight:"Forgetting God is never passive; it always walks somewhere else."},
{q:"What would their land become?", options:["Desolate, and a perpetual hissing","A fruitful garden","A highway for traders","A pasture for flocks"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:16",verse:"To make their land desolate, and a perpetual hissing; every one that passeth thereby shall be astonished, and wag his head.", insight:"A land that hissed at God would be hissed at by passersby."},
{q:"How would God scatter them?", options:["As with an east wind before the enemy","As chaff before the whirlwind","As sheep without a shepherd","As dust in the storm"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:17",verse:"I will scatter them as with an east wind before the enemy; I will shew them the back, and not the face, in the day of their calamity.", insight:"The east wind strips everything bare; a scattering like that leaves nothing to rebuild on."},
{q:"What face would God show in their calamity?", options:["The back, and not the face","His anger, but not His mercy","His face, but turned away","Neither back nor face"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:17",verse:"I will scatter them as with an east wind before the enemy; I will shew them the back, and not the face, in the day of their calamity.", insight:"To lose God's face is to lose everything; His turned back is the darkest judgment of all."},
{q:"What did the plotters say would not perish?", options:["The law from the priest, counsel from the wise, and the word from the prophet","The gold from the temple","The throne of David","The walls of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:18",verse:"Then said they, Come, and let us devise devices against Jeremiah; for the law shall not perish from the priest, nor counsel from the wise, nor the word from the prophet. Come, and let us smite him with the tongue, and let us not give heed to any of his words.", insight:"They claimed God's institutions would survive while plotting against God's prophet; religion without repentance is a shell."},
{q:"What does Jeremiah pray?", options:["Give heed to me, O LORD, and hearken to the voice of them that contend with me","Hide me in the cleft of the rock","Send fire from heaven","Let me flee to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:19",verse:"Give heed to me, O LORD, and hearken to the voice of them that contend with me.", insight:"The prophet takes his enemies' case to the only court that matters."},
{q:"What had they done to Jeremiah?", options:["Digged a pit for his soul","Set him in the stocks","Driven him from the city","Stoned him at the gate"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:20",verse:"Shall evil be recompensed for good? for they have digged a pit for my soul. Remember that I stood before thee to speak good for them, and to turn away thy wrath from them.", insight:"They dug a pit for the man who stood before God to turn away wrath from them; that is how deep ingratitude goes."},
{q:"What does Jeremiah ask God to do with his enemies' sin?", options:["Not forgive their iniquity, nor blot out their sin, but overthrow them","Forgive them seventy times seven","Forget their trespasses","Turn them from their evil"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 18:23",verse:"Yet, LORD, thou knowest all their counsel against me to slay me: forgive not their iniquity, neither blot out their sin from thy sight, but let them be overthrown before thee; deal thus with them in the time of thine anger.", insight:"Some prayers in Scripture are honest cries, not patterns to copy; even so, God heard him."}
]},
{n:19, name:"The Valley of Slaughter", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 19:11",verse:"And shalt say unto them, Thus saith the LORD of hosts; Even so will I break this people and this city, as one breaketh a potter’s vessel, that cannot be made whole again: and they shall bury them in Tophet, till there be no place to bury."}, facts:["Did you know Tophet in the valley of Hinnom was where children were burned to Baal? God says it never even entered His mind, and renames it the valley of slaughter (Jeremiah 19:5-6).","Did you know Jeremiah's sermon prop was a clay bottle smashed before the elders: 'even so will I break this people and this city, as one breaketh a potter's vessel, that cannot be made whole again' (Jeremiah 19:10-11)?","Did you know the rooftop worship of the host of heaven, plain for all to see, is named as the reason the houses of Jerusalem would be defiled (Jeremiah 19:13)?"], questions:[
{q:"What was Jeremiah told to get?", options:["A potter's earthen bottle","A new clay vessel","A scroll and ink","A basket of figs"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:1",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, Go and get a potter’s earthen bottle, and take of the ancients of the people, and of the ancients of the priests;", insight:"A cheap clay bottle would carry the costliest sermon; God loves humble props."},
{q:"Whom was he to take with him?", options:["The ancients of the people, and the ancients of the priests","The king and his princes","The captains of the army","The singers of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:1",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, Go and get a potter’s earthen bottle, and take of the ancients of the people, and of the ancients of the priests;", insight:"The elders had to watch; judgment is harder to ignore when you see it coming."},
{q:"Where was he to proclaim the words?", options:["The valley of the son of Hinnom, by the entry of the east gate","The court of the temple","The pool of Siloam","The hill of the skull"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:2",verse:"And go forth unto the valley of the son of Hinnom, which is by the entry of the east gate, and proclaim there the words that I shall tell thee,", insight:"The sermon was preached at the scene of the crime; Tophet would hear its own sentence."},
{q:"What would happen to whoever heard?", options:["His ears shall tingle","His heart shall melt","His knees shall tremble","His eyes shall weep"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:3",verse:"And say, Hear ye the word of the LORD, O kings of Judah, and inhabitants of Jerusalem; Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Behold, I will bring evil upon this place, the which whosoever heareth, his ears shall tingle.", insight:"Some news is so dreadful the body hears it before the mind does."},
{q:"With what had they filled this place?", options:["The blood of innocents","The ashes of sacrifices","The gold of idols","The bones of kings"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:4",verse:"Because they have forsaken me, and have estranged this place, and have burned incense in it unto other gods, whom neither they nor their fathers have known, nor the kings of Judah, and have filled this place with the blood of innocents;", insight:"Innocent blood cries from the ground; God never files it away and forgets."},
{q:"What had they built to Baal?", options:["High places, to burn their sons with fire for burnt offerings","A temple of cedar and gold","Altars of uncut stone","A grove of tall oaks"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:5",verse:"They have built also the high places of Baal, to burn their sons with fire for burnt offerings unto Baal, which I commanded not, nor spake it, neither came it into my mind:", insight:"They gave their children to a god who could give nothing back; idolatry always collects its cruelest tax."},
{q:"What did God say about this horror?", options:["Which I commanded not, nor spake it, neither came it into my mind","Which I permitted for a season","Which the fathers also did","Which the nations taught them"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:5",verse:"They have built also the high places of Baal, to burn their sons with fire for burnt offerings unto Baal, which I commanded not, nor spake it, neither came it into my mind:", insight:"This evil never came from God's heart and never entered His mind; do not blame heaven for hell's ideas."},
{q:"What would the place no more be called?", options:["Tophet, nor the valley of the son of Hinnom","The valley of blessing","The gate of the east","The field of the potter"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:6",verse:"Therefore, behold, the days come, saith the LORD, that this place shall no more be called Tophet, nor The valley of the son of Hinnom, but The valley of slaughter.", insight:"God renames places to match their judgment; Tophet's old name would not survive what was coming."},
{q:"What new name would it bear?", options:["The valley of slaughter","The valley of weeping","The valley of desolation","The valley of silence"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:6",verse:"Therefore, behold, the days come, saith the LORD, that this place shall no more be called Tophet, nor The valley of the son of Hinnom, but The valley of slaughter.", insight:"The place of child sacrifice would become the place of slaughter; sin's harvest always matches its seed."},
{q:"What would God make void?", options:["The counsel of Judah and Jerusalem in this place","The covenant with David","The laws of Moses","The feasts of the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:7",verse:"And I will make void the counsel of Judah and Jerusalem in this place; and I will cause them to fall by the sword before their enemies, and by the hands of them that seek their lives: and their carcases will I give to be meat for the fowls of the heaven, and for the beasts of the earth.", insight:"Clever plans mean nothing when God voids them; counsel without God is just noise."},
{q:"What would their carcases become?", options:["Meat for the fowls of the heaven, and for the beasts of the earth","Dust upon the mountains","Ashes in the valley","Food for the dogs of the city"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:7",verse:"And I will make void the counsel of Judah and Jerusalem in this place; and I will cause them to fall by the sword before their enemies, and by the hands of them that seek their lives: and their carcases will I give to be meat for the fowls of the heaven, and for the beasts of the earth.", insight:"Those who fed their children to Baal would feed the birds; judgment mirrors the crime."},
{q:"How would the city be described?", options:["Desolate, and an hissing","Strong and fortified","Beautiful and peaceful","Rich and full of people"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:8",verse:"And I will make this city desolate, and an hissing; every one that passeth thereby shall be astonished and hiss because of all the plagues thereof.", insight:"Passersby would hiss where worshippers once sang; ruin becomes its own sermon."},
{q:"What horror would the siege bring?", options:["They would eat the flesh of their sons and daughters","They would eat the temple sacrifices","They would eat their horses and mules","They would eat bread made of straw"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:9",verse:"And I will cause them to eat the flesh of their sons and the flesh of their daughters, and they shall eat every one the flesh of his friend in the siege and straitness, wherewith their enemies, and they that seek their lives, shall straiten them.", insight:"Starvation turns family into food; this is where rebellion against God finally leads."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah to do with the bottle?", options:["Break it in the sight of the men that go with thee","Fill it with water from the brook","Set it down at the east gate","Give it to the ancients"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:10",verse:"Then shalt thou break the bottle in the sight of the men that go with thee,", insight:"A sermon you can hear shatter stays with you; God made the judgment visible."},
{q:"What did the broken bottle mean?", options:["Even so will I break this people and this city","Even so will I humble the proud","Even so will I scatter the nations","Even so will I cleanse the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:11",verse:"And shalt say unto them, Thus saith the LORD of hosts; Even so will I break this people and this city, as one breaketh a potter’s vessel, that cannot be made whole again: and they shall bury them in Tophet, till there be no place to bury.", insight:"The bottle preached what words alone could not: this breaking would be total."},
{q:"What made the breaking final?", options:["As one breaketh a potter's vessel, that cannot be made whole again","As a branch cut from the vine","As a garment torn in two","As a wall broken down"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:11",verse:"And shalt say unto them, Thus saith the LORD of hosts; Even so will I break this people and this city, as one breaketh a potter’s vessel, that cannot be made whole again: and they shall bury them in Tophet, till there be no place to bury.", insight:"Unlike the marred vessel in chapter 18, this one could not be remade; some refusals run out of mercy."},
{q:"What would the city become as?", options:["As Tophet","As Sodom","As a wilderness","As a ruin forever"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:12",verse:"Thus will I do unto this place, saith the LORD, and to the inhabitants thereof, and even make this city as Tophet:", insight:"Jerusalem, the holy city, would be like the valley of child sacrifice; holiness lost is worse than never having it."},
{q:"Why would the houses be defiled?", options:["Because upon their roofs they burned incense unto the host of heaven and poured drink offerings","Because they were built with stolen timber","Because the priests had abandoned them","Because the people had fled from them"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:13",verse:"And the houses of Jerusalem, and the houses of the kings of Judah, shall be defiled as the place of Tophet, because of all the houses upon whose roofs they have burned incense unto all the host of heaven, and have poured out drink offerings unto other gods.", insight:"Rooftop idolatry was public sin; God treats open rebellion as open defiance."},
{q:"Where did Jeremiah stand after Tophet?", options:["In the court of the LORD's house","At the gate of Benjamin","On the Mount of Olives","In the king's palace"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:14",verse:"Then came Jeremiah from Tophet, whither the LORD had sent him to prophesy; and he stood in the court of the LORD’S house; and said to all the people,", insight:"From the valley of slaughter to the house of God, the prophet carried the same word everywhere."},
{q:"Why would God bring all this evil?", options:["Because they hardened their necks, that they might not hear His words","Because they were poor and weak","Because the nations demanded it","Because the temple was too small"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 19:15",verse:"Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Behold, I will bring upon this city and upon all her towns all the evil that I have pronounced against it, because they have hardened their necks, that they might not hear my words.", insight:"A stiff neck is a chosen posture; they could have bowed, and would not."}
]},
{n:20, name:"Terror on Every Side", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 20:9",verse:"Then I said, I will not make mention of him, nor speak any more in his name. But his word was in mine heart as a burning fire shut up in my bones, and I was weary with forbearing, and I could not stay."}, facts:["Did you know Pashur, the priest who struck Jeremiah, got a new name from the prophet: Magor-missabib, meaning terror on every side, a terror to himself and all his friends (Jeremiah 20:3-4, 10)?","Did you know Jeremiah once resolved to quit preaching, but God's word was 'as a burning fire shut up in my bones,' and he could not stay silent (Jeremiah 20:9)?","Did you know this chapter holds both one of the Bible's highest praises, 'Sing unto the LORD,' and its bitterest lament, Jeremiah cursing the day he was born, only verses apart (Jeremiah 20:13-14)?"], questions:[
{q:"Who was Pashur?", options:["The son of Immer the priest, chief governor in the house of the LORD","The captain of the king's guard","A scribe of the temple","A prophet of Samaria"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:1",verse:"Now Pashur the son of Immer the priest, who was also chief governor in the house of the LORD, heard that Jeremiah prophesied these things.", insight:"The man who struck Jeremiah was not a pagan but a priest; opposition often wears religious robes."},
{q:"What did Pashur do to Jeremiah?", options:["Smote him, and put him in the stocks","Cast him into a dungeon","Drove him from the city","Set him before the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:2",verse:"Then Pashur smote Jeremiah the prophet, and put him in the stocks that were in the high gate of Benjamin, which was by the house of the LORD.", insight:"Truth gets struck before it gets heard; the prophet's body paid for the prophet's message."},
{q:"What new name did Jeremiah give Pashur?", options:["Magor-missabib","Shear-jashub","Maher-shalal-hash-baz","Lo-ammi"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:3",verse:"And it came to pass on the morrow, that Pashur brought forth Jeremiah out of the stocks. Then said Jeremiah unto him, The LORD hath not called thy name Pashur, but Magor-missabib.", insight:"God renames people to match their future; Pashur's new name was a sentence."},
{q:"What did the new name mean for Pashur?", options:["I will make thee a terror to thyself, and to all thy friends","Thou shalt be a blessing to the nations","Thou shalt live long in the land","Thou shalt see peace upon Israel"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:4",verse:"For thus saith the LORD, Behold, I will make thee a terror to thyself, and to all thy friends: and they shall fall by the sword of their enemies, and thine eyes shall behold it: and I will give all Judah into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he shall carry them captive into Babylon, and shall slay them with the sword.", insight:"A terror to himself: the cruelest judgment is to become what you feared."},
{q:"What would the enemies take to Babylon?", options:["All the strength of the city, and all the labours thereof, and all the precious things","Only the vessels of the temple","Only the king's household","The flocks and the herds"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:5",verse:"Moreover I will deliver all the strength of this city, and all the labours thereof, and all the precious things thereof, and all the treasures of the kings of Judah will I give into the hand of their enemies, which shall spoil them, and take them, and carry them to Babylon.", insight:"Everything the city trusted in would ride off in enemy carts; misplaced trust always gets carried away."},
{q:"What would happen to Pashur?", options:["He would go into captivity, come to Babylon, and there die and be buried","He would flee to Egypt and live","He would repent and be restored","He would be made king in Judah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:6",verse:"And thou, Pashur, and all that dwell in thine house shall go into captivity: and thou shalt come to Babylon, and there thou shalt die, and shalt be buried there, thou, and all thy friends, to whom thou hast prophesied lies.", insight:"The priest who silenced God's prophet would die far from God's house."},
{q:"Why would this happen to Pashur?", options:["Because he had prophesied lies to his friends","Because he refused to pay tribute","Because he fled from battle","Because he stole from the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:6",verse:"And thou, Pashur, and all that dwell in thine house shall go into captivity: and thou shalt come to Babylon, and there thou shalt die, and shalt be buried there, thou, and all thy friends, to whom thou hast prophesied lies.", insight:"False prophecy is not a small sin; it steers souls wrong, and God holds the steering."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah's complaint?", options:["Thou hast deceived me, and I was deceived: I am in derision daily","Thou hast forsaken me in the pit","Thou hast hidden Thy face from me","Thou hast broken Thy covenant"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:7",verse:"O LORD, thou hast deceived me, and I was deceived: thou art stronger than I, and hast prevailed: I am in derision daily, every one mocketh me.", insight:"Even a great prophet can feel tricked by his calling; honest complaint is still prayer."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah cry?", options:["Violence and spoil","Peace and safety","Woe and lamentation","Judgment and mercy"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:8",verse:"For since I spake, I cried out, I cried violence and spoil; because the word of the LORD was made a reproach unto me, and a derision, daily.", insight:"His message never changed with his mood; he preached violence and spoil while feeling them himself."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah resolve?", options:["I will not make mention of him, nor speak any more in his name","I will flee to a far country","I will hide in the wilderness","I will serve the king instead"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:9",verse:"Then I said, I will not make mention of him, nor speak any more in his name. But his word was in mine heart as a burning fire shut up in my bones, and I was weary with forbearing, and I could not stay.", insight:"Every faithful servant has a quitting day planned; the question is whether God lets you keep it."},
{q:"What stopped him?", options:["His word was in mine heart as a burning fire shut up in my bones, and I could not stay","The king commanded him to continue","His friends persuaded him","He feared punishment"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:9",verse:"Then I said, I will not make mention of him, nor speak any more in his name. But his word was in mine heart as a burning fire shut up in my bones, and I was weary with forbearing, and I could not stay.", insight:"God's word will not stay buried; it burns its way out of every silent prophet."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah hear on every side?", options:["Fear on every side","Peace on every side","Songs on every side","Weeping on every side"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:10",verse:"For I heard the defaming of many, fear on every side. Report, say they, and we will report it. All my familiars watched for my halting, saying, Peradventure he will be enticed, and we shall prevail against him, and we shall take our revenge on him.", insight:"The prophet lived inside his own prophecy; Magor-missabib was his address too."},
{q:"What did his familiars watch for?", options:["His halting, to take revenge on him","His preaching, to learn from him","His coming, to welcome him","His words, to write them down"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:10",verse:"For I heard the defaming of many, fear on every side. Report, say they, and we will report it. All my familiars watched for my halting, saying, Peradventure he will be enticed, and we shall prevail against him, and we shall take our revenge on him.", insight:"The loneliest place is among friends who wait for you to fall."},
{q:"Who was with Jeremiah?", options:["The LORD as a mighty terrible one","An angel with a drawn sword","The king's guard","A company of prophets"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:11",verse:"But the LORD is with me as a mighty terrible one: therefore my persecutors shall stumble, and they shall not prevail: they shall be greatly ashamed; for they shall not prosper: their everlasting confusion shall never be forgotten.", insight:"One mighty companion outweighs a crowd of persecutors; fear flees when God stands near."},
{q:"What does Jeremiah ask to see?", options:["Thy vengeance on them","Thy mercy toward them","Thy face in the temple","Thy hand upon the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:12",verse:"But, O LORD of hosts, that triest the righteous, and seest the reins and the heart, let me see thy vengeance on them: for unto thee have I opened my cause.", insight:"He opens his cause to God instead of taking revenge himself; that is what the righteous do with their anger."},
{q:"What burst of praise breaks through?", options:["Sing unto the LORD, praise ye the LORD: for he hath delivered the soul of the poor","Blessed be the name of the LORD forever","Great is the LORD, and greatly to be praised","Holy, holy, holy is the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:13",verse:"Sing unto the LORD, praise ye the LORD: for he hath delivered the soul of the poor from the hand of evildoers.", insight:"Praise in the middle of persecution is defiance; Jeremiah sings before the rescue is finished."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah curse?", options:["The day wherein he was born","The city of Jerusalem","The name of Pashur","The throne of David"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:14",verse:"Cursed be the day wherein I was born: let not the day wherein my mother bare me be blessed.", insight:"The same mouth that sang in verse 13 curses in verse 14; Scripture lets broken men be inconsistent."},
{q:"Whom else did he curse?", options:["The man who brought tidings of his birth, making his father very glad","The priest who struck him","The king who ignored him","The friends who betrayed him"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:15",verse:"Cursed be the man who brought tidings to my father, saying, A man child is born unto thee; making him very glad.", insight:"Sorrow can turn even a father's joy into a target; grief does not think straight."},
{q:"Why did he wish he had died in the womb?", options:["That his mother might have been his grave","That he might have seen the LORD","That he might have escaped Babylon","That he might have been a priest"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:17",verse:"Because he slew me not from the womb; or that my mother might have been my grave, and her womb to be always great with me.", insight:"He wished for the grave before the grief; despair shrinks a prophet's whole life to its pain."},
{q:"What was his final question?", options:["Wherefore came I forth out of the womb to see labour and sorrow?","Wherefore hast Thou forsaken me?","Wherefore do the wicked prosper?","Wherefore is the city destroyed?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 20:18",verse:"Wherefore came I forth out of the womb to see labour and sorrow, that my days should be consumed with shame?", insight:"The chapter ends without an answer; some questions are carried, not solved."}
]}
]);
